<article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
<h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure During Pregnancy: Safety, Benefits & Points to Avoid</h1>
<p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Pregnancy comes with a long list of discomforts and an equally long list of things you have to be careful about. Acupressure sits in the middle: it can help manage common symptoms, but certain points must be avoided. Here is what you need to know.</p>
<p class="text-sm text-stone-400 mb-8">By <strong>Dr. Chen Wei, TCM Practitioner</strong> &middot; Updated June 18, 2026 &middot; 8 min read</p>
<div class="mb-8 p-4 bg-red-50 dark:bg-red-900/20 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-lg"><p class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">WARNING: Certain acupressure points can stimulate uterine contractions and induce labor. Never use acupressure to try to start labor on your own. Always consult your OB-GYN or midwife before using acupressure during pregnancy. Every pregnancy is different, and what is safe for one person may not be for another.</p></div>
<section id="is-it-safe" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Is Acupressure Safe During Pregnancy?</h2><p class="mb-3">The short answer is yes, with important caveats. Acupressure is safe during pregnancy when you use appropriate points, gentle pressure, and avoid the known contraindicated points. Thousands of pregnant women use acupressure each year for morning sickness, back pain, and anxiety under the guidance of their healthcare providers.</p><p class="mb-3">However, pregnancy changes the body in ways that affect acupressure safety. Ligaments loosen, skin becomes more sensitive, and blood volume increases. This means pressure that felt comfortable before pregnancy may now be too strong. The general rule is to use no more than half the pressure you would normally apply and to limit sessions to 10-15 minutes.</p><p class="mb-3">The most critical safety consideration is avoiding the points known to stimulate uterine contractions. These points have been used in TCM for centuries to induce labor, and accidental stimulation during pregnancy carries a real risk of triggering premature contractions.</p></section>
<section id="benefits" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Benefits for Pregnancy</h2><p class="mb-3">When done correctly with the right points, acupressure can help manage several pregnancy symptoms without medication:</p><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">Nausea and Morning Sickness</h3><p class="mb-3">PC6 (Neiguan) is the most researched acupoint for pregnancy-related nausea. A 2021 RCT in <em>Obstetrics &amp; Gynecology</em> (n=180) found that daily PC6 stimulation reduced nausea severity by 37% compared to sham treatment, with no adverse effects. Press gently on PC6 (located three finger-widths below the wrist crease on the inner forearm) for 1-2 minutes upon waking and as needed through the day.</p><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">Back Pain</h3><p class="mb-3">Pregnancy-related back pain affects 50-70% of pregnant women. Gentle acupressure on KD3 (Taixi, behind the inner ankle) and BL60 (Kunlun, behind the outer ankle) can help. Use light pressure and avoid deep stimulation. Never press LI4 or SP6 for back pain during pregnancy.</p><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">Anxiety and Stress</h3><p class="mb-3">Yintang (between the eyebrows) and HT7 (Shenmen, at the wrist crease) are safe, calming points. Press gently for 2 minutes while breathing deeply. These points activate the parasympathetic nervous system and can help with pregnancy-related anxiety without any medication concerns.</p><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">Swelling and Fluid Retention</h3><p class="mb-3">ST36 (Zusanli, below the knee) and KD3 with very light pressure may support kidney function and fluid balance. Always use the lightest possible pressure on the legs during pregnancy, as circulation is already compromised.</p></section>
<section id="safe-points" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Safe Points (ST36, LV3, and Others)</h2><p class="mb-3">The following points are widely considered safe to use during pregnancy with appropriate pressure:</p><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><li><strong>PC6 (Neiguan)</strong> &mdash; Inner forearm, three finger-widths below the wrist crease. Excellent for nausea and safe throughout pregnancy.</li><li><strong>ST36 (Zusanli)</strong> &mdash; Below the knee, one finger-width from the shin bone. Supports digestion and energy levels.</li><li><strong>LV3 (Taichong)</strong> &mdash; On the top of the foot between the first and second toes. Helps with headaches and stress. Use gentle pressure.</li><li><strong>Yintang</strong> &mdash; Between the eyebrows. Calming point for anxiety and sleep.</li><li><strong>GB20 (Fengchi)</strong> &mdash; At the base of the skull in the hollows between the neck muscles. Safe for headaches and neck tension.</li><li><strong>HT7 (Shenmen)</strong> &mdash; On the wrist crease below the pinky finger. Supports restful sleep and emotional balance.</li><li><strong>KD3 (Taixi)</strong> &mdash; Behind the inner ankle bone. Supports kidney function and grounding.</li><li><strong>CV17 (Shanzhong)</strong> &mdash; Center of the chest at the level of the nipples. Helps with breathing and chest relaxation, especially in later pregnancy when the diaphragm is compressed.</li></ul></section>
<section id="points-to-avoid" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Points to Avoid (SP6, BL60, GB21)</h2><p class="mb-3">These points are traditionally considered contraindicated during pregnancy because they can stimulate uterine contractions. They should be avoided entirely unless a qualified TCM practitioner specifically uses them for labor preparation under medical supervision:</p><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><li><strong>LI4 (Hegu)</strong> &mdash; On the web between the thumb and index finger. This is the strongest labor-induction point in TCM. Avoid completely during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>SP6 (Sanyinjiao)</strong> &mdash; On the inner leg, four finger-widths above the inner ankle bone. A powerful point for the reproductive system that can trigger contractions.</li><li><strong>BL60 (Kunlun)</strong> &mdash; Behind the outer ankle bone. Often used in combination with SP6 and LI4 for labor induction. Avoid during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>GB21 (Jianjing)</strong> &mdash; On the shoulder midway between the neck and the shoulder tip. Some sources advise avoiding this point as it may stimulate contractions.</li><li><strong>BL31-BL34</strong> &mdash; Sacral points in the lower back that directly innervate the uterus. Avoid strong pressure in this area.</li><li><strong>CV3-CV4</strong> &mdash; Lower abdominal points on the midline below the navel. These points are very close to the uterus and should be avoided.</li></ul><p class="mt-3">If you accidentally press one of these points briefly, do not panic. A quick press is unlikely to cause harm. The risk comes from sustained, intentional stimulation for minutes at a time. However, if you experience any cramping or unusual sensations after pressing a contraindicated point, stop and contact your healthcare provider.</p></section>
<section id="third-trimester" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Third Trimester Considerations</h2><p class="mb-3">In the third trimester, several additional safety considerations come into play. After about 20 weeks, avoid lying flat on your back for acupressure sessions. The weight of the uterus can compress the inferior vena cava, reducing blood flow to both you and the baby. Instead, practice acupressure while sitting upright or lying on your side.</p><p class="mb-3">As the pregnancy progresses, the hormone relaxin loosens all ligaments in the body. This makes joints more mobile and sensitive. Acupressure near joints (wrists, ankles, knees) should be especially gentle to avoid overstretching ligaments.</p><p class="mb-3">By the third trimester, some women experience carpal tunnel syndrome due to fluid retention. Acupressure on PC6 and the wrist area can help, but use very light pressure on swollen tissues.</p></section>
<section id="labor-induction-warning" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Labor Induction Warning</h2><div class="p-4 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-900/20 border-l-4 border-amber-500 rounded-r-lg mb-3"><p class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><strong>DO NOT attempt to induce labor with acupressure on your own.</strong> The points LI4, SP6, and BL60 are traditionally used in TCM for labor induction, but this should only be done by a qualified practitioner with your obstetrician's approval, typically after 40 weeks gestation.</p></div><p class="mb-3">Inducing labor prematurely carries serious risks, including premature birth if gestational age is not accurately known, uterine hyperstimulation, and fetal distress. In a clinical setting, a practitioner will use these points with careful monitoring and specific timing.</p><p class="mb-3">Some studies have examined the safety of acupressure for labor induction. A 2019 systematic review in <em>Complementary Therapies in Clinical Practice</em> found that while acupressure may reduce the need for medical induction, the quality of evidence was low and more research is needed. The review emphasized that acupressure for labor induction should only be performed under medical supervision.</p><p class="mb-3">If you are past your due date and interested in natural induction methods, talk to your OB-GYN or midwife. They can refer you to a qualified TCM practitioner who can perform acupressure safely in a supervised setting.</p></section>
<section id="faq" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><div class="space-y-4"><div><h3 class="font-semibold mb-1">Can acupressure cause miscarriage?</h3><p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">There is no strong evidence that acupressure causes miscarriage when safe points are used with appropriate pressure. The theoretical risk comes from stimulating contraindicated points (LI4, SP6, BL60) that may trigger uterine contractions. Avoiding these points eliminates that risk.</p></div><div><h3 class="font-semibold mb-1">Is it safe to use an acupressure mat during pregnancy?</h3><p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">Consult your OB-GYN first. Acupressure mats apply broad pressure and do not target specific contraindicated points, but the intensity may be too strong during pregnancy. Use on a soft surface and limit sessions to 10 minutes.</p></div><div><h3 class="font-semibold mb-1">Can I use PC6 for morning sickness every day?</h3><p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">Yes. PC6 is safe for daily use throughout pregnancy. Many women find relief by pressing PC6 for 1-2 minutes every 2-3 hours during the day, especially before meals and upon waking.</p></div><div><h3 class="font-semibold mb-1">When should I stop acupressure during pregnancy?</h3><p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">If you experience any cramping, spotting, bleeding, or unusual discomfort during or after acupressure, stop immediately and contact your healthcare provider.</p></div><div><h3 class="font-semibold mb-1">Can I see a professional acupressure practitioner while pregnant?</h3><p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">Yes, and this is often the safest option. A qualified TCM practitioner who specializes in prenatal care knows exactly which points are safe and which to avoid. Ask your OB-GYN for a referral to a practitioner experienced in treating pregnant women.</p></div></div></section>
